{"requirements":[{"id":"cron_access","requirement":"On node1, allow only root and opsadmin to schedule cron jobs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final /etc/cron.allow contains exactly root and opsadmin, is owned by root:root with mode 0600, and persisted across restart. Direct crontab access checks produced normal “no crontab” responses for root and opsadmin while nobody was explicitly rejected, both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-18266d1051b64863","cmd-b1defb827ebc43cc","cmd-1802d1aa4e654358","cmd-c52a156035354d1e"]},{"id":"platform_preservation","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"AlmaLinux 9.6 and kernel 5.14.0-570.12.1.el9_6 were identical before and after execution and restart. The existing EL9 cronie packages were used; the timeline shows no package installation or upgrade, and final package health remained clean.","evidence_ids":["cmd-521d6cdc107e448b","cmd-18266d1051b64863","cmd-c52a156035354d1e","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating opsadmin and atomically installing /etc/cron.allow in cmd-104b02968b9643b2 directly established the requested policy. Temporary files used by cmd-b1defb827ebc43cc were removed. The scheduled reboot in cmd-70c93b5da93f45eb was required by the provider-wide restart protocol and successfully completed. Final observations show no failed services, package damage, new exposure, or abandoned temporary residue; socket and systemd-private-directory differences are normal consequences of the verified reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-104b02968b9643b2","cmd-b1defb827ebc43cc","cmd-70c93b5da93f45eb","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied. The independent restart protocol is also complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, the reboot was acknowledged, the connection dropped during reboot, a different post-restart boot ID was observed, and system, crond, policy data, account, and enforcement recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["Authorization for root and opsadmin was demonstrated with `crontab -l`, which returned the expected “no crontab” state, rather than by installing and removing an actual scheduled job. The explicit allow-list and contrasting denial for nobody make the access-control conclusion strong.","The post-restart password-status output for opsadmin is malformed/redacted, so the executor’s claim that its password is locked is not independently established; password locking was not part of the public task contract."]}
